In .NET 10 we had the sync-over-async opt-in configured. What breaks in .NET 11?

data.content:
**In .NET 11, sync I/O on the Cosmos DB provider is fully removed — no opt-in exists anymore.** Your `.ToList()`, `.First()`, `.Count()`, and `.SaveChanges()` calls will **always throw** at runtime.

In .NET 10 you could re-enable sync-over-async; in EF Core 11 that escape hatch is gone.

**Fix — convert to async:**

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

This will ripple `async/await` up through your callers. There is no workaround — the Cosmos SDK is async-only, and EF Core 11 no longer wraps it.

**Bonus:** You also have a second Cosmos-specific behavioral change — empty owned collections now return an empty collection instead of `null`. If any of your code null-checks owned collections, switch to `.Count == 0`.
